# Line-ending policy.
#
# This file exists because of a real bug, not as boilerplate. `docker/create-topics.sh` was
# authored on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, so Git rewrote it to CRLF on checkout. The
# script is bind-mounted into a Linux container and run by bash, which reads the trailing
# `\r` as part of each command and fails with:
#
#     /create-topics.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#
# kafka-init then exits 2, no topics are created, and every service starts up looking
# perfectly healthy while consuming nothing — the same silent-failure shape ARCHITECTURE.md
# 8.4 describes for missing topics.
#
# It was invisible to whoever committed it, because the file is LF in the working tree until
# Git converts it on the *next* checkout. It breaks for the next person to clone, which is
# the worst way for a bug to be distributed.

# Normalise text files to LF inside the repository. Working-tree endings still follow the
# developer's platform, which is what most people want for source they edit in an IDE.
* text=auto

# ...except for anything a Linux container executes or parses. These must be LF in the
# working tree too, on every machine, regardless of core.autocrlf.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
docker-compose.yml text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.jar   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
